	Contributing

Read PLAN first. It says what is being built next and what the constraints are.
If your change contradicts a rule in there, adding a dependency, introducing a
thread, pulling in AndroidX, open an issue before opening a patch.

docs/ARCHITECTURE says how the pieces fit together.


Getting a build
---------------

	git clone https://github.com/bardia049b-stack/Vm.git
	cd Vm
	make -j"$(nproc)"
	make test
	./rvm --help

A C11 compiler and GNU make. dtc is only needed for make dtb, lcov only for
make coverage, and the Android SDK only for the APK.


Before you send it
------------------

	make lint                  -Werror syntax pass over src and tests
	./scripts/test.sh --werror the same, optimised, which is not the same
	make MODE=sanity test      ASan and UBSan
	make format                clang-format, needs LLVM 15

make lint is -fsyntax-only and does not see warnings that only appear once the
compiler folds constants at -O2. The --werror run does. CI does both.

shellcheck -x tools/*.sh scripts/*.sh has to be clean, including at info level.
Use find rather than ls for globbing, quote expansions inside ${VAR#"$prefix"},
and write { [ a ] || [ b ]; } instead of [ a -o b ].


Style
-----

.clang-format is the policy. LLVM base, 4 spaces, 100 columns, attached braces,
right aligned pointers. Run make format before committing. CI pins clang-format
to LLVM 15.0.0 because that is what formatted the tree, and a different major
version produces diffs.

-Wall -Wextra -Wshadow -Wundef -Wpointer-arith -Wcast-qual -Wstrict-prototypes
-Wmissing-prototypes are on for every build. Every non static function needs a
prototype in a header.

Commit messages: imperative subject, no period, and say why in the body. The
diff already says what. If you fixed a decode or trap bug, include the trace
line or the objdump output that proved it.


Adding a test
-------------

Declare the suite in tests/test_suites.h and add one RUN() line in
tests/test_main.c. The Makefile globs tests/*.c, so nothing else changes.

The harness builds a real machine. Emit guest code with th_emit(), or
th_emit16() for compressed forms, set up registers, then th_run_all(). It runs
until the PC reaches the end of the emitted stream, so helper instructions from
th_li() cannot throw off a step count.

Three things that have already cost time here.

th_li() zero extends to 32 bits.

th_run_all() does not stop on STEP_TRAP. An all zero register file after a
block means the guest trapped and looped, not that the arithmetic was wrong.

Derive expected values from the spec text, not from memory of the ISA.


Adding a device
---------------

Write src/devices/foo.c and foo.h, following virtio_blk.c for the shape. Attach
it to a virtio slot in vm_new() and give it an IRQ with RVM_VIRTIO_IRQ(n).

Add the node to both dtb/rvm.dts and src/loader/fdt.c. They have to stay
identical:

	./rvm --dump-dtb /tmp/gen.dtb
	dtc -I dtb -O dts /tmp/gen.dtb > /tmp/a
	dtc -I dtb -O dts dtb/rvm.dtb  > /tmp/b
	diff /tmp/a /tmp/b

android/jni/CMakeLists.txt globs src/*.c with CONFIGURE_DEPENDS, so a new file
is picked up with no build system change.

Test the transport behaviour, not just the device logic.


What gets sent back
-------------------

A new dependency of any kind, including a vendored .c from another project.

A lock in src/. The VM is single threaded and the only shared state in the
project is the JNI input ring and vm.running.

An allocation on the hot path.

AndroidX, Material Components, Compose or a WebView.

An APK over 5 MB. CI fails the build, not just a check.

A commit that reformats code it did not otherwise touch.

phandle = <N> written by hand in a .dts.


Pull requests
-------------

One idea per request. A patch that fixes a bug and refactors the file it lives
in is two patches.

Say what the change does, why the old behaviour was wrong, and how you checked
it. build, test, lint and android all have to be green.


Security
--------

A guest escape is a security bug, not a correctness bug. See SECURITY, and
please do not open a public issue for it.
